<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marija Gimbutas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a>–<a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Marija Birutė Alseikaitė-Gimbutienė</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marija Gimbutas was a Lithuanian archaeologist and anthropologist known for her research into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> cultures of Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and for her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurgan">Kurgan</a> hypothesis, which located the Proto-Indo-European homeland in the Pontic Steppe. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, she fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> before the advancing Soviet army and moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where she was professor at UCLA from 1963 to 1989 and directed excavations of Neolithic sites in southeastern Europe. Her Goddess books described a goddess-centred Old Europe supplanted by patriarchal Kurgan invaders; mainstream archaeology dismissed these later works, while three genetic studies in 2015 gave support to her Kurgan theory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marija-gimbutas.jpg" alt="Marija Gimbutas" width="330" height="298" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Monica Boirar · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prof-Dr-Marija-Gimbutas-Copyright-Foto-Monica-Boirar-aka-Monica-Beurer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Marija Birutė Alseikaitė was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> to Veronika Janulaitytė-Alseikienė and Danielius Alseika, members of the Lithuanian intelligentsia. Her mother held a doctorate in ophthalmology from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and her father a medical degree from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Dorpat</a>. Her parents were connoisseurs of Lithuanian folk art and received writers and scholars, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jonas-basanavicius">Jonas Basanavičius</a>, whose collected folklore remained important to her. The family settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> in 1931, and after her father's sudden death five years later she resolved to become a scholar. From 1936 she joined ethnographic expeditions to record folklore and studied Lithuanian beliefs and rituals of death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>She studied linguistics at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-magnus-university">Vytautas Magnus University</a> from 1938, then archaeology, ethnology and folklore at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, where she took her master's degree in 1942. In 1941 she married the architect Jurgis Gimbutas. With a young daughter she fled the advancing Soviet army to areas held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and in 1946 she received a doctorate from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-tubingen">University of Tübingen</a> for a study of prehistoric burial rites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>.</p><p>In the 1950s the family moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. She worked at Harvard, becoming a Fellow of the Peabody Museum in 1955, and then moved to UCLA, where she was Professor of European Archaeology and Indo-European Studies and became Curator of Old World Archaeology in 1965.</p><p>In 1956 she proposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurgan">Kurgan</a> hypothesis, which joined the archaeology of burial mounds to linguistics to explain the origin and migrations of the Proto-Indo-European speakers. Between 1967 and 1980 she directed excavations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> sites in southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, including Anzabegovo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a> and Sitagroi and Achilleion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Her Bronze Age Cultures of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> appeared in 1965.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Her last three English-language books, The Goddesses and Gods of Old Europe (1974), The Language of the Goddess (1989) and The Civilization of the Goddess (1991), described a goddess-centred, peaceful Old Europe that she held was overturned by patriarchal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurgan">Kurgan</a> warriors. Joseph Campbell admired the work, and the ecofeminist Charlene Spretnak later argued that a backlash against it had been organised.</p><p>Many archaeologists rejected it: Bernard Wailes judged her knowledgeable but weak in critical analysis, and David W. Anthony, who praised her view of the Indo-European homeland, noted that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> had hillforts and weapons long before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurgan">Kurgan</a> peoples. Three genetic studies of 2015 were taken to support the Kurgan theory. She died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> in 1994.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marija Gimbutas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Modern scholars, from Lubor Niederle and Aleksander Gieysztor to Ivanov, Toporov, <b>Marija Gimbutas</b> and Rybakov, have rebuilt the religion by comparison with other Indo-European traditions and from 19th- and 20th-century folklore, though some of these studies are contested.</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>How the languages spread is debated: <b>Gimbutas</b> saw a military victory, Anthony proposed elite recruitment, and the authors of Haak et al.</blockquote><a href="/culture/corded-ware-culture">Corded Ware culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Marija Gimbutas</b> based her theory of a peaceful, goddess-centred Old Europe partly on these figurines; her conclusions were always controversial and are now rejected by many scholars.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cucuteni-trypillia-culture">Cucuteni–Trypillia culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The LBK people settled on river terraces with fertile loess and grew a mix of crops in small plots, a “garden type of civilization” in <b>Gimbutas</b>’s phrase.</blockquote><a href="/culture/linear-pottery-culture">Linear Pottery culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Marija Gimbutas</b> placed the culture within her “Old Europe”, which she described as relatively homogeneous, peaceful and matrifocal.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vinca-culture">Vinča culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>In her Kurgan hypothesis <b>Marija Gimbutas</b> identified the Yamnaya with the late Proto-Indo-Europeans, and this hypothesis is widely accepted.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yamnaya-culture">Yamnaya culture · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.69,25.28&amp;year=1968" aria-label="Vilnius on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5106 292 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Marija Gimbutas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5556 562h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5556 562h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.69,25.28&amp;year=1968">What was here in 1968? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
